      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-7663",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "When we have an inequality involving an exponential function with a positive base, we're allowed to multiply or divide both sides by the exponential function.\n\nMultiplying both sides of the given inequality by $11^x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({x}^{2}-8)^{3}}{{11}^{x}} & \\ge 0 \\\\ {11}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{({x}^{2}-8)^{3}}{{11}^{x}} & \\ge {11}^{x}\\cdot 0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-8)^{3} & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the inequality using familiar methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}-8)^{3}} & \\ge \\sqrt[3]{0} \\\\ {x}^{2}-8 & \\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ge 8 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & \\ge \\sqrt{8} \\\\ |x| & \\ge 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality is $x \\leq -2\\sqrt 2$ or $x \\geq 2 \\sqrt 2.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013930/example-7663.json",
          "file_sha256": "c2d14d802e3e42b8048405534c58b3ecdca34dca37517db6d089fa04c39db5ea"
        }
