    {
      "question": "e-7664",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "53f4ae20a57e9e06a9139aa9b96920ce250e7e7807a51bce6524ce2d51e23119",
      "new_sha256": "9acf0d7ded08dbf9fcb4edb3dc16f206f71a8403f2c1636a829a5ab4cbab2d16",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-7664",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we move all the terms to the left side of the inequality and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x+1}-2{e}^{x+1} & <-x{e}^{x+1} \\\\ {x}^{2}{e}^{x+1}+x{e}^{x+1}-2{e}^{x+1} & <0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+x-2){e}^{x+1} & <0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1){e}^{x+1} & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we divide both sides by $e^{x+1}$ and get\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-1) <   0.\n$$\n\nThis represents an upwards parabola whose roots are at $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ The parabola is below the $x$-axis when $-2 <   x <   1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013930/assets/2f01104ae0cb9a41c5c5e4b8393e346f7d0e9b2bfd1afa593ee6bdfb0fff39f2.png)\n\nTherefore, the solution is $-2 <   x <   1.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013930/example-7664.json",
          "file_sha256": "e2448705d42b4a0fcebf2e24d60d7adee2351045a2007ab01ec4de356a4ed42b"
